WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.140
Monitor the workload from user request to hardware.

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:07.926
Track availability, errors, queue time, latency, and throughput alongside GPU utilization,

00:00:07.926 --> 00:00:10.580
VRAM, CPU, RAM, disk, and network use.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:15.090
Low utilization on an expensive accelerator is a signal to investigate data loading,

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:16.899
runtime configuration, or demand.

00:00:16.899 --> 00:00:21.285
High VRAM use with allocation errors may require a smaller model, shorter context,

00:00:21.285 --> 00:00:22.780
different batch, or larger rental.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:26.920
Health checks should prove that the model can answer, not merely that a process exists.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:31.172
For longer jobs, save checkpoints and outputs to durable storage on a defined schedule.

00:00:31.172 --> 00:00:34.890
Back up irreplaceable configuration and data, then test restoration.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:38.345
Do not rely on one instance disk as the only copy of completed work.

00:00:38.345 --> 00:00:40.790
Provider capacity can disappear between sessions,

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.280
and interruptible machines may stop during a run.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.120
Maintain an acceptable alternate GPU type or region when deadlines matter.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.270
Design clients to handle transient failure and retries safely.

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:54.335
Rebuild a replacement from documented scripts or images rather than repairing a unique

00:00:54.335 --> 00:00:55.480
machine indefinitely.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.420
A rental should be replaceable by design.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.405
Control change with versioned configurations and rollback plans.

00:01:00.405 --> 00:01:04.900
A new driver, container, runtime, model revision, or quantization can change memory use,

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:06.360
output, and performance.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.920
Test changes on limited work before adopting them.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:13.025
During an incident, isolate access, revoke credentials, preserve appropriate evidence,

00:01:13.025 --> 00:01:16.330
restore a known-good environment, and account for residual resources.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:20.650
Reliable rental operations mean the job can survive an instance loss and the team can end

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.920
the session without losing results or leaving exposure behind.
